# Capacity Is a Lookup. Timing Is a Discipline.

The Monday after the deal cleared, twelve development offices sent letters.

Eleven of them were postmarked New Haven, Cambridge, Palo Alto, and a couple of small brick buildings in Providence. Their format was almost identical — the founder's name over a warm one-paragraph congratulations, a two-paragraph reminder of the school's giving priorities for the coming year, and a call-to-schedule paragraph offering three windows in the second half of September.

They arrived on a small oak counter in Atherton on a Wednesday morning. Ben — thirty-eight, four kids, still wearing the same navy fleece he wore to a demo day in the December of 2018 — sorted the mail while the kettle boiled. He set eleven of the twelve to one side of the sugar bowl, unopened.

He opened the twelfth.

The twelfth was a plain cream envelope with a real 68-cent stamp and a return address in a town of nine thousand people in west-central Illinois. The handwriting on the front was blue Papermate. The letter inside was one page. It was from the assistant dean of admissions at the small state college Ben transferred out of after his sophomore year, in the March of 2007, when the tuition line at his mother's kitchen table stopped making sense.

He read it twice. Then he called his wife over. Then he read it to her.

## The letter he opened was on his calendar in June

Here is the thing that matters, and it is the whole thing.

The eleven letters were written on Sunday night, after the acquisition press release ran on Sunday afternoon. The one letter was written on the fourteenth of June, seven weeks before the deal was announced, on a Tuesday morning in a small basement office, by a woman named Deborah — fifty-four, twenty-nine years at the same institution, second cup of coffee — who reads, every Monday morning, the SEC 8-K filings for any privately held AI company whose board list, cap table, or LinkedIn bios include the phrase *transfer credit* alongside her school's name.

Deborah reads eight or nine of these a week. Most turn out to be nothing. She writes to the founder anyway, in her own hand, once a season, whether anything happens or not. She has been doing this since a Tuesday morning in the February of 2004.

Ben's name has been on her list since a Monday in the June of 2019, when a piece of paperwork Ben's Series A lawyer filed with the Delaware Secretary of State — as boring a document as the sector produces — quietly named a board member whose GitHub bio still, in 2026, reads *transfer from a small state school to Berkeley, 2005–2007.*

Deborah did not write to Ben the day she found the filing. She noted the name. She wrote to him the following November, a warm two-paragraph note about the school's aviation program, which Ben's older brother Michael had graduated from in 2001. Ben wrote back. He has written back to every letter Deborah has sent since.

She wrote him again on the fourteenth of June, 2026. It was the forty-first letter.

## Capacity is what you buy. Timing is what you do.

For twenty years, one category of software has been sold — at increasingly expensive annual contracts, to institutional advancement offices that already had the meeting — to answer the question, *of our four hundred thousand alumni, whose name should be on the vice president's calendar this fall?*

The tool answers it well. It prices Ben at capacity band four of five, philanthropic likelihood eighty-first percentile, recommended ask twenty-five million over five years. It renders the answer as a color of dot on a portfolio dashboard on the third floor of a converted stone building in New Haven on a Monday afternoon in July.

The color changed on the Sunday of the press release.

The letter had been written six weeks before.

Capacity is a lookup. Any vendor with a Bloomberg seat, a Pitchbook subscription, and a filings scraper can hand you a color of dot on any founder in the country by Friday. The industry has bought this lookup, in various forms, for two decades. The lookup is real. The lookup is not the job.

The job is timing. And timing is a discipline. It is a Tuesday morning at seven-forty, in a basement office in a town the sector has never heard of, reading the filings that the Ivy shop will not open until the Sunday after the press release. Every eight-figure gift the sector has closed in the last three years was on someone's calendar three weeks before the check arrived. Usually the wrong someone's.

## What Deborah's Tuesday morning looks like

Deborah's tool is not expensive. It is a browser tab, a spreadsheet, and thirty years of paying attention. She has three saved SEC EDGAR searches. She has a Google Alert on twelve alumni names. She has a list of 137 board memberships she rechecks in April and October for a name she recognizes.

She writes one letter a day. Some Mondays she writes two.

She does not send a mail merge. She does not maintain a moves-management pipeline the way the sector's consultants would like her to. She does not have a wealth-screen contract. She does not attend CASE. She has, in the last twelve years, closed nineteen seven-figure gifts for a school whose entire development office is four people, including her.

The category leaders have not, in twelve years, sold Deborah a single license. She is the wrong customer profile. The sales rep — bright, thirty, quota to hit by September — is coached to skip past mid-Illinois state schools with four-person shops on the way to a medium-sized private research university with a hundred-person team and a nine-month sales cycle.

The sales rep is not making a mistake. She is optimizing for the pipeline the vendor was capitalized to build. And the vendor was capitalized to build the pipeline that sells the *capacity lookup* — the color of dot — because a color of dot is a report a hundred-person team knows how to run.

Nobody, so far, has built the timing tool.

## The tool we are trying to build for Deborah

The timing tool sits on Deborah's laptop at seven-forty on a Tuesday morning and — quietly, in the ten minutes before her second cup — hands her the three names whose calendars this month want her Papermate blue.

Not the ones with the biggest capacity band. The ones whose filings ran last week. The ones whose brother-in-law's tenure decision came down on Friday. The ones whose kid just landed the early-decision letter she has been quietly rooting for. The ones whose small liquidity event closes on the Thursday after Labor Day. The ones who, this month, are inside a real moment that would not survive being scheduled for a September window.

The tool knows what a good listener would know. It writes the small careful sentences a Tuesday-morning assistant dean would write, if the Tuesday morning had thirty hours in it instead of eight. It leaves the letter to her.

That is Rōmy. It is a smaller tool than the industry has been selling for two decades. It is smaller because it is not trying to replace Deborah. It is trying to give her back her Tuesdays.

## For the officer reading this on a phone at 8:17 a.m.

You have — pull up your alumni file and count — around two hundred and sixty former students whose current LinkedIn lists a company you have not heard of. Twenty-three of them are founders. Six of them will have a liquidity event before Christmas. One of them, in the next eighteen months, will be an eight-figure gift you get right if you were on his calendar in September and get wrong if you find out on a Sunday.

The color-of-dot report your vendor renewed in April will not tell you which of the six. It cannot tell you which one. That is not what you bought.

The letter is the whole thing. The letter has to be on the counter on the Wednesday morning after — because it was written on the Tuesday morning before.

## One small thing to try this week

Pick one name off your alumni file. Not the top-of-band prospect. The founder whose company you can barely pronounce, whose LinkedIn reads *stealth,* whose file has one line — *transfer credit, spring 2007, GPA 3.4* — and no engagement notes.

Read him for twenty minutes. His last podcast. His co-founder's Substack. The filings, if there are filings. The name of his older brother, if the family shows up in the alumni magazine index.

Then, on Wednesday morning, from your kitchen table, on a plain cream envelope with a real stamp — write one page in your own hand. Ask him nothing. Tell him one small true thing about the year he was on your campus.

Sign it. Mail it.

Sit with the small warm notion that the letter you just wrote will, in some quiet way you may never learn about, be on his counter on a Wednesday morning in a year like 2029 — sorted to the side of the sugar bowl, opened first.

Deborah has been doing it since 2004.

You can start on Wednesday.
